 oustics-of-accented-labor-genevieve-nnaji-and-industrial-authorship-in-lio
 nheart-2018-rejoice-abutsa-doctoral-candidate-cornell-university\n\nBlack 
 women’s presence\, participation\, and labor have been central to Nollyw
 ood’s formation and global circulation\, even as their contributions rem
 ain marginalized in critical scholarship. On-screen and behind the scenes\
 , women have shaped Nollywood’s emergence as the world’s second-larges
 t film industry by annual production volume. Situated at the intersection 
 of African cultural production\, women’s cinema\, and global Black media
 \, this talk examines women’s paradoxical positioning within Nigerian ci
 nema through one of Africa’s most prominent film stars\, Genevieve Nnaji
 . Her directorial debut\, Lionheart (2018)\, raises critical questions abo
 ut women’s creative labor\, authorship\, and industrial politics\, parti
 cularly how women filmmakers navigate\, challenge\, and transform the inst
 itutional structures that determine African cinema’s visibility and legi
 timacy on the global stage. Combining textual and industrial analysis\, th
 e talk examines how Lionheart deploys a multilingual acoustic landscape to
  explore the politics of language\, listening\, and belonging. In doing so
 \, I demonstrate how Nnaji’s aesthetic and industrial interventions illu
 minate the complex relationship between women’s creative authority\, Nig
 erian cultural identity\, and the global circulation of African cinema.\n\
 nRejoice Abutsa is a filmmaker\, programmer\, and doctoral candidate in th
 e Department of Performing and Media Arts at Cornell University. Working a
